      },
      "worked": "For the first law, let $x\\in(A\\cup B)'$. Then $x\\notin A\\cup B$, so $x\\notin A$ and $x\\notin B$. Hence $x\\in A'$ and $x\\in B'$, giving $x\\in A'\\cap B'$. Therefore $(A\\cup B)'\\subseteq A'\\cap B'$.\n\nConversely, let $x\\in A'\\cap B'$. Then $x\\notin A$ and $x\\notin B$, so $x\\notin A\\cup B$. Thus $x\\in(A\\cup B)'$, giving $A'\\cap B'\\subseteq(A\\cup B)'$. Both inclusions establish $(A\\cup B)'=A'\\cap B'$.\n\nFor the second law, let $x\\in(A\\cap B)'$. Then $x\\notin A\\cap B$, so $x\\notin A$ or $x\\notin B$. Hence $x\\in A'$ or $x\\in B'$, giving $x\\in A'\\cup B'$. Therefore $(A\\cap B)'\\subseteq A'\\cup B'$.\n\nConversely, let $x\\in A'\\cup B'$. Then $x\\notin A$ or $x\\notin B$, so $x\\notin A\\cap B$. Thus $x\\in(A\\cap B)'$, giving $A'\\cup B'\\subseteq(A\\cap B)'$. Both inclusions establish $(A\\cap B)'=A'\\cup B'$.",
